it circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\pi }{4}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-321100" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-321100" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-321100" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-{120}^{∘}"] ["math" "-{150}^{∘}"] ["math" "-{180}^{∘}"] ["math" "-{60}^{∘}"] ["math" "-{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117408" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-{90}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{30}^{∘}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nSince ${30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) & =3\\cdot ({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{120}^{∘}"] ["math" "{135}^{∘}"] ["math" "{180}^{∘}"] ["math" "{270}^{∘}"] ["math" "{90}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117459" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{90}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in degrees, $\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(-{30}^{∘})=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $-{30}^{∘}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3})={-30}^{∘}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-320730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-30"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by:\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}<\\theta <{90}^{∘}\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{60}^{∘}=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{∘}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot ({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{10}^{∘}"] ["math" "{12}^{∘}"] ["math" "{15}^{∘}"] ["math" "{20}^{∘}"] ["math" "{9}^{∘}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-117458" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{20}^{∘}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Expressed in radians, $\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=${{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\sqrt{3}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where the tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\ntan\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\narctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}arctan\\,(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320789" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-320789" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-320789" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{6}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $1$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where tangent is positive.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions, we know that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is an angle in the first quadrant that lies within the range of $arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "c2affa56a7b3908a997343832a437005a4cc286057c7e6dc1be2001b32bcc917"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "q-24757" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/state.json" "file_sha256" "9680257bbfd9cb8e41823111f00ab857ea9132a14d813c4ce3b817cbe66d0e99"} {"question" "e-6754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-6754.json" "file_sha256" "1046849a0250333f4ddc90c646133181eb24b87374281fc4a3c28c29f92f4bb1"} {"question" "e-6754" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-6754.json" "file_sha256" "1046849a0250333f4ddc90c646133181eb24b87374281fc4a3c28c29f92f4bb1"} {"question" "e-7220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-7220.json" "file_sha256" "ddf59bf37fb261e092e472e14fd01d23a8bc9245a1f751991323279de7e7e790"} {"question" "e-7220" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-7220.json" "file_sha256" "ddf59bf37fb261e092e472e14fd01d23a8bc9245a1f751991323279de7e7e790"} {"question" "e-6755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-6755.json" "file_sha256" "3c50abe2033c890d9e1e47bf591ecbd8a1fef70fbdc84a806476fabefa01771c"} {"question" "e-6755" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/example-6755.json" "file_sha256" "3c50abe2033c890d9e1e47bf591ecbd8a1fef70fbdc84a806476fabefa01771c"}] "usage" {"q-320730" [] "e-7220" [] "e-6755" [] "q-24770" [] "e-6754" [] "q-321269" [] "q-82178" []} "basis" 1247 "retractions" [[17592186269035 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n{0}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where cosine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘})=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\narccos\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})={45}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186261854 :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$"] [17592186261854 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$"] [17592186261854 :question/answer-fields 17592186275051] [17592186060168 :question/problem "What is $\\arcsin (-\\frac{1}{2})$?"] [17592186059533 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)$."] [17592186059533 :question/worked-solution "The range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arccos x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta \\le \\pi\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nis negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/14075e642ddf4d45f6ee40cea7dbb087.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\n$$"] [17592186059544 :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right)$."] [17592186059544 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arcsin x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/209/Source/Images/cbdba1490f8dd76396ad73dfea0fe152.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\n$$\n\nHowever,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\ndoes not lie within the range of $\\theta$. To get an angle that's coterminal with\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nand lies within the range of $\\theta$, we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"] [17592186059534 :question/problem "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3)$."] [17592186059534 :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of\n\n$$\n\\theta = \\arctan x\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that\n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nis an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan$, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186058007 17592186058008 17592186058009 17592186058010 17592186058011 17592186269037 17592186273833 17592186273834 17592186273835 17592186273836 17592186273837 17592186278585] "immutable_fields" [[17592186058006 :answer-field/correct] [17592186058006 :answer-field/id] [17592186058006 :answer-field/key] [17592186058006 :answer-field/type] [17592186269036 :answer-field/correct] [17592186269036 :answer-field/id] [17592186269036 :answer-field/key] [17592186269036 :answer-field/type] [17592186275051 :answer-field/correct] [17592186275051 :answer-field/id] [17592186275051 :answer-field/key] [17592186275051 :answer-field/type] [17592186278584 :answer-field/correct] [17592186278584 :